    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_02fd06dd8c20a8f84201f98f6ccca2f955887f5c33353f2a0cae4b92c0298a34",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: for sigma>1, log|zeta(sigma+it)| has nonnegative prime-power weights times cos(t log n). Applying 3+4cos theta+cos 2theta>=0 coefficientwise gives a three-frequency zeta-product lower bound. If zeta(1+it) vanished for nonzero t, suitable powers of sigma-1 make the pole and a zero of any positive multiplicity comparable: the left side stays bounded while the right diverges. Interpretation: the trigonometric polynomial couples frequencies 0,t,2t so oscillation cannot collectively cancel the boundary pole. Boundary: positivity is used only right of one and exact product exponents are scan-sensitive.",
