    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_76161931667dc0f25226cf0aa562819541cb27c232f7f6bc68942dfc76f7fabf",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: a finite Dirichlet sum plus a bounded fractional-part tail expresses zeta for sigma>0. The bound contains competing finite-sum, endpoint, and t-weighted tail costs; choosing X=t yields O(log t) for sigma>=1 and O_delta(t^{1-delta}) for sigma>=delta>0. Interpretation: truncation is a tunable interface between coefficients and continuation, and matching length to vertical frequency balances rather than eliminates the two costs. Boundary: these are coarse estimates, constants depend on delta, and no critical-line-strength claim follows.",
