    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_04f0289c8e0f15f3e22d716a54862f8d070206aa03daa836f06e94ce76e21420",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Reciprocal Gaussian structure drives two opposite translations. In Hardy's route, Poisson self-duality gives theta reciprocity; Mellin completion, evenization, and integration by parts turn it into an even cosine kernel whose inversion and high moments convert eventual critical-line sign into derivative rigidity at a strip boundary. In Hamburger's converse route, an assumed completed functional equation is Mellin-inverted into reciprocal Gaussian sums with a residue correction; a second Gaussian transform produces a periodic meromorphic expression whose translated pole residues label and force successive Dirichlet coefficients to be equal.",
