    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3e58eb213812201c0d251a69fe15deeec75c1491997e41e6c986c11853c695e0",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Boundary nonvanishing is precisely what lets the Mellin-Laplace transform -zeta'(s)/(s zeta(s)) have only the residue-one pole at s=1 on the critical boundary. Wiener-Ikehara subtracts that pole, controls localized boundary averages, and uses monotonicity of psi to recover psi(x)~x. Conversely, subtracting x from psi removes the main pole; if the remaining error is o(x), its scaled transform tends to zero at every boundary point, which is incompatible with the nonzero residue produced by any zeta zero there.",
