    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_7ff208f6ebbae8fd89803beb127f5f8b1d747b78c1c1fec27922381956bad291",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Completion first turns zeta's meromorphic zero question into a symmetric entire function xi. Stirling bounds then show log M(r) is asymptotic to one-half r log r, so xi has order one but not finite type; the earlier finite-order theory converts that growth into convergence exponent one, infinitely many zeros, a genus-one Hadamard product, and logarithmic-derivative partial fractions. Thus analytic continuation becomes discrete zero structure only after growth supplies the missing constraint on the zero-free exponential factor.",
