    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_89878f2198589c8f7b02ae122f90b59a7ff9fa9045c934234ae54a9a2364fdbe",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The two boundary-zero proofs use positivity in different ways. The first applies a nonnegative trigonometric polynomial coefficientwise to the positive prime-power expansion of log zeta, coupling frequencies 0, t, and 2t so a boundary zero cannot offset the pole at one. The second engineers a zeta quotient with squared-modulus Dirichlet coefficients; positivity forces a singularity at its convergence boundary, while a hypothetical conjugate zero pair would cancel the numerator's pole and analytically continue the quotient to an impossible boundary and interior value.",
